# =============================================================================
# Script-level Receipt template (machine-readable JSONL: one JSON object per line)
# Concept owner: "kernel/K12 Quality Assurance/07 Audit Evidence Reuse and Invalidation.md"
#   -- This file is the lightweight receipt schema produced by deterministic
#      check scripts; the full K12/07 AuditReceipt (with scope /
#      acceptance_predicate / the three fingerprint kinds etc.) is completed
#      by the AuditPlan layer when entering the Audit Receipt Register; a
#      script receipt's receipt_id can be referenced directly as
#      evidence_ref / gate_receipts.
# Note: real receipts files are pure JSONL and contain no # comment lines;
#      the # lines in this template exist for documentation only. Field meanings:
#   receipt_id     : "audit-<tool>-<UTC timestamp>-<per-invocation random token>-<sequence>", append-only protocol ID, never reused (K12/07: receipt_id: audit-<stable-id>)
#   check          : check item identifier, e.g. "link-missing" / "vocab-unknown-value" / "proof-zero-field"
#   target         : checked object (vault-relative path[:line] or field name)
#   result         : pass / fail / candidate (candidate = produces candidates only, requires human judgement; see K10/04 Automated Language Review Boundary)
#   details        : detail message stating the concrete evidence; must not just say "QA passed" (K12/07)
#   checked_at     : UTC ISO8601 check time
#   tool           : producing script name (counterpart of K12/07 verifier.name)
#   tool_version   : script version (counterpart of K12/07 verifier.version; incompatible parser/verifier changes trigger Direct Invalidation)
#   gate_id        : stable K00/12 Gate ID emitted by registered deterministic
#                    gate producers; historical producer versions may lack it
#                    and remain history-only, never current gate input
#   invalidated_by : invalidation source (successor receipt_id or invalidation event description); null while valid (K12/07 Invalidation)
# Receipt JSONL is append-only. A writer never restores an older register image
# over concurrent appends; a partial or otherwise uncertain append preserves
# its runtime writer lock for restart reconciliation.
# Trust boundary: tool/actor/reviewer fields are declared labels, SHA-256 binds
# bytes but is not a signature, and append-only/immutable describe the supported
# protocol. A party controlling the repository, tools, and all evidence can
# fabricate an internally consistent history; stronger provenance needs an
# external signature, protected runner, or attestation service.
# Optional tool-specific fields may extend this lightweight layer. In
# `check_residual_content.py` receipts:
#   scan_id            : stable ID from the selected profile's Registered Scan Registry
#   config_fingerprint : `sha256:<hex>` over the exact profile-owned config bytes; null when the config could not be read or validated
# `check_freshness.py` 2.1.0 emits one page-level `freshness` candidate per
# active candidate outcome and exactly one `freshness-check-summary` for every
# completed scan. Page candidates carry `freshness_schema_version`, `scan_id`,
# stable path-derived `candidate_id`, `candidate_kind`, typed reason fields,
# `as_of`, effective volatility/source, baseline/review-by, and overdue days.
# The summary carries the same schema/scan identity plus `scan_complete`, the
# closed classification counts, page and scan finding counts, sorted
# `candidate_records` / `candidate_ids`, `candidate_set_basis` and
# `candidate_set_sha256`, normalized scope/exclusions, the exact defaults
# source/fingerprint, and `input_snapshot_sha256` over the bytes and semantic
# mtimes actually classified. A zero-file scan is a scan-level candidate with
# `scan_finding_codes: ["nothing_checked"]`; it has no synthetic page record.
# Only a completed scan with an empty candidate set has `result: pass`.
# A passing `check_profile.py` 2.2.0 `profile-load` summary carries:
#   selected_profile_manifest      : canonical `profiles/<id>/profile.md` identity
#   profile_snapshot_sha256        : path-sensitive SHA-256 over the selected manifest plus its typed Profile-owned dependency closure
#   profile_contract_fingerprint   : SHA-256 over the canonical typed dependency graph rooted at that manifest
#   profile_load_inputs_sha256      : SHA-256 over the canonical root-owned Profile-load inputs
# The four fields occur together only on `profile-check-summary` pass
# evidence after the complete contract is authorized. A partial readable graph
# is diagnostic state, never a fingerprint-bearing Gate pass.
# Both the explicit `--positive-controls-only` invocation and the following
# production invocation of a registered K12/09 item-6 verifier finish with a
# reliable summary carrying:
#   positive_control_result      : `passed`
#   positive_control_mode        : `production-classifier`
#   positive_control_count       : positive integer number of executed controls
#   positive_control_fingerprint : `sha256:<64 lowercase hex>` over the exact
#                                  verifier-owned control set
# These shared evidence fields do not prescribe the verifier's control-input
# representation. `check_batch_close.py` 1.12.0 requires the two final pass
# summaries to agree exactly on tool, tool_version, check, scan_id,
# config_fingerprint, and every positive_control_* field. Each scan_id must
# also equal the admitted Registered Scan ID; when that row declares an
# explicit config, each config_fingerprint must equal the SHA-256 of those
# admitted bytes. Two identical self-reports cannot replace that comparison.
# `check_queue.py` pass receipts:
#   task_id                       : frozen task identity
#   queue_revision                : structural Queue revision
#   queue_state_revision          : lifecycle/hold revision
#   required_queue_sha256         : SHA-256 over the exact Queue bytes checked
#   remaining_required_work_units : non-terminal Required work-unit count
#   queue_check_mode              : consistency, require-ready:<id>,
#                                   require-revalidation:<batch-id>,
#                                   require-complete,
#                                   require-maintenance-complete, or resume-status
#                                   (the complete set, bound to checker version;
#                                   `check_queue.py` argparse is its owner)
# A current `require-revalidation:<batch-id>` aggregate uses
# `mapping_protocol_version: owner-projection-v1`. It records the original
# `required_gate_ids` plus `mapped_owner_gate_ids`, `immediate_gate_ids`,
# `native_owner_gate_ids`, and `deferred_native_owner_gate_ids`. The
# `due_gate_ids`, `deferred_to_later_transition_gate_ids`, and
# `unrepeatable_passed_gate_ids` partition the mapped owner set at the target
# batch state. `boundary_gate_receipts` contains exactly the due owner receipts;
# a raw semantic-leaf receipt is never accepted there. Each
# `revalidation_bindings` row binds `affected_gate_id`, `required_gate_id`,
# `mapped_owner_gate_id`, `owner_claim_edge`, `mapping_protocol_version`,
# `claim_disposition`, `gate_receipt_id`, and the adoption/boundary/invalidated-
# evidence identities from which that claim was derived. A native-owner row is
# deferred to its ordinary transition rather than satisfied early by the
# aggregate. Producer-era replay preserves aggregates written before this
# projection and never rewrites their binding shape.
# A successful `consistency` receipt also carries
# `repository_snapshot_sha256`: a path-sensitive SHA-256 over every regular
# repository file except the root `.git/` and `.cambium/` namespaces. The
# batch-close aggregator, every member of its producer-era Closed List, and its distinct
# global-review receipt bind that same value as `merged_snapshot_sha256`.
# `update_queue.py` recomputes it immediately before close; matching receipt
# strings without matching current repository bytes cannot authorize close.
# `check_corpus_plan.py` pass receipts carry
# `gate_id: corpus-plan-structure` and bind the exact selected Profile
# manifest, Profile Scope, Corpus Planning slot, Global Map, Capability Matrix,
# and Gap Register paths and SHA-256 values; task identity; Queue revisions; all three
# canonical state SHA-256 values; applicability; and the repository snapshot.
# Any bound byte or runtime revision change makes the receipt stale. A batch-
# close aggregator explicitly records `corpus_plan_required`, the sorted
# trigger set (`R13` and/or `manifest`), and either a distinct current
# `corpus_plan_receipt` or null. Manifest applicability is exact intersection
# with the selected Profile manifest, Scope, slot, artifacts, and validator-parsed Global Map entry,
# Matrix canonical/evidence, and Gap promoted/evidence paths; it never follows
# prose or inferred semantic relationships.
# `record_corpus_acceptance.py` appends a fresh structural receipt followed by
# one distinct `gate_id: corpus-plan-semantic-acceptance` receipt. The latter
# carries acceptance_id, acceptance_plan_path/SHA-256, authority_role_id,
# actor_role_id, decision_scope_id, structural_check_receipt, and the exact
# ordered capability_decisions. It repeats the structural byte/runtime binding
# so a Profile, artifact, canonical-state, Queue-revision, plan, authority, or
# repository-snapshot change makes the semantic acceptance stale. The decision
# plan lives only under `.cambium/deltas/corpus-plan-acceptances/`; no Markdown
# report is a receipt input or output.
# Examples of current stable-Gate producer protocols -- not the complete set;
# the Stable Gate ID Registry in K00/12 is the only complete enumeration of
# registered Gate IDs and their producers: check_profile 2.2.0 / profile-load;
# check_links 1.6.0 / wiki-link-integrity; check_vocab 1.8.0 /
# frontmatter-vocabulary and priority-quota-distribution;
# check_residual_content 1.2.0 /
# registered-residual-content; check_queue 1.25.0 / its registered
# Required-Queue modes; check_batch_close 1.12.0 / batch-close; check_proof
# 1.17.0 / terminal-proof; and adopt_standards 1.8.0 /
# standards-adoption. Deterministic producers not shown above -- among them
# check_corpus_plan and record_corpus_acceptance -- follow the same rule. Every receipt
# created by one of these producer versions carries that exact Gate ID. The
# Stable Gate ID Registry's Tool/Tool-version/Check/Mode tuple selects the
# canonical summary or aggregator from the producer's diagnostic/member
# receipts and fails closed on a version mismatch.
# Every registered Gate producer additionally binds the current Required Queue
# identity -- `task_id`, `standards_version`, `selected_profile_manifest` --
# read from `.cambium/state/required_queue.yaml`. Equality is necessary for
# current authorization but does not make a raw semantic leaf into a Standards-
# revalidation boundary owner; that projection is owned by K00/12's capability
# registry. `adopt_standards.py` binds the planned post-adoption
# identity, since its receipts are built before the state write and consumed
# after it. A run outside any Cambium runtime (no `.cambium/state/`) omits the
# three fields rather than writing null: an omitted field claims nothing, while
# an explicit null would satisfy consumers that only test field presence.
# Public R01 `check_queue.py` 1.25.0 admission reruns the complete
# `profile-load` closure. The lower-level runtime view used to construct a
# corrective Standards adoption retains only the selected Profile's
# identity/sentinel guard, so an invalid current Profile does not prevent a
# transaction whose candidate after-image passes full `profile-load`.
# A passing `require-ready:*` receipt additionally carries
# `activation_protocol`, the Task Contract, reading plan, read-back plan and
# Card Bundle SHA-256 values, plus the closed `activation_bundle_manifest`. It
# stores paths and content hashes, never Card text.
#   card-first-phased-readback-v4 (current): the manifest carries the exact
#     deliverable records and their frozen phase plan. Admission delivers no
#     content. Phase parts travel through `deliver-phase:*` and are acknowledged
#     through `ack-activation-phase:*`; K13/20 consumes exact-set evidence.
#   card-first-readback-v3 (sealed history): the manifest carries one piece per
#     deliverable and uses the legacy piece delivery/ack operations. Admission
#     also claims no delivery.
#   card-first-readback-v1 / v2 (sealed history): the manifest carries `cards`
#     and `startup_readbacks`, the tool result carries transient
#     `activation_delivery_payload` bytes, and admission itself recorded
#     `machine-delivered`/`degraded`. Those eras replay under their own shape
#     and never acquire the later fields.
# `deliver-activation-piece:<batch>:<piece>` carries an `activation-piece-v1`
# delivery: `piece_id`, `piece_sha256`, `piece_envelope_bytes`,
# `delivery_attempt_id` and a single-use `delivery_nonce`, with the exact file
# bytes only in the transient `activation_piece_payload`. The server re-reads
# current bytes and refuses a source that drifted since admission, and refuses
# any delivery serializing above the Tool-owned activation envelope budget.
# `ack-activation-piece:<batch>:<piece>` carries an `activation-piece-ack-v1`
# record binding the delivery receipt, its nonce, `delivery_attempt_id` and the
# answering `execution_context_id`. An ack proves this context consumed that
# delivery; on its own it does not prove the body ahead of the nonce entered
# the model context. Only the server hash, a conformant Host Adapter (see
# `Tools/host-conformance.yaml`), and this ack together are piece delivery
# evidence.
# `deliver-readback:*` carries a
# `card-readback-addendum-v1` manifest, parent Bundle hash, and registered rule;
# its exact source bytes exist only in transient `readback_delivery_payload`.
# `open -> merge-ready` additionally consumes one current in-batch wrapper:
#   tool                    : manual-attestation
#   tool_version            : 1.0.0
#   gate_id                 : batch-review
#   check                   : batch_gate
#   target / batch_id       : exact Queue batch ID
#   task_id                 : current Task ID
#   delta_page_receipt_ids  : sorted unique union of every Delta page's
#                             gate_receipts
# Page receipts may be valid historical evidence. They never authorize the
# lifecycle edge without this current wrapper, and invalidated IDs are rejected.
# When the selected Profile registers Batch Review Requirements the wrapper
# additionally binds the frozen judgment set:
#   review_requirement_set_sha256 : the expansion hash the activation froze
#   judgment_receipt_ids          : sorted unique profile_batch_judgment IDs
#   judgment_record_set_sha256    : hash over the exact actual records
# Each member receipt is produced by `record_batch_judgment` (check:
# profile_batch_judgment) and binds batch_id, judgment_item_id,
# target_selector, receipt_schema, reviewer_role, the batch's current
# activation as opening_transition_receipt, the target's
